#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518954 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,-1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(2 u + v\\right)^{2}\\\\\\left(u - v\\right)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\left(2 u + v\\right)&4 \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =(8 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(4 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} + 108 u^{2} v - 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-2,-1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-2,v=-1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(- (-1) + (-2)\\right)^{2} \\left((-1) + 2 (-2)\\right)\\\\& =180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|180|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =180\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518955 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,-1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}2 u v^{3}\\\\u^{2} + v^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =\\begin{vmatrix}2 v^{3}&6 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&2 v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =(2 v^{3})\\cdot (2 v)-(6 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 12 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{4}\\\\& =- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(-2,-1)} & =\\left(- 4 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=-2,v=-1}\\\\& =- 4 (-1)^{2} \\left(- (-1)^{2} + 3 (-2)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-44|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =44\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518958 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(3,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}3 u v^{3}\\\\u^{2} + 2 v^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =\\begin{vmatrix}3 v^{3}&9 u v^{2}\\\\2 u&4 v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =(3 v^{3})\\cdot (4 v)-(9 u v^{2})\\cdot (2 u)\\\\& =- 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 12 v^{4}\\\\& =- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(3,1)} & =\\left(- 6 v^{2} \\left(3 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=3,v=1}\\\\& =- 6 (1)^{2} \\left(- 2 (1)^{2} + 3 (3)^{2}\\right)\\\\& =-150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-150|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =150\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518966 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,-1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(2 u + v\\right)^{2}\\\\\\left(u - v\\right)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))\\approx\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\cdot\\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\left(2 u + v\\right)&4 \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}&- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\& =(8 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (- 3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})-(4 \\left(2 u + v\\right))\\cdot (3 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2})\\\\& =- 72 u^{3} + 108 u^{2} v - 36 v^{3}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\\\\\left.\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(1,-1)} & =\\left(- 36 \\left(u - v\\right)^{2} \\left(2 u + v\\right)\\right)\\big|_{u=1,v=-1}\\\\& =- 36 \\left(- (-1) + (1)\\right)^{2} \\left((-1) + 2 (1)\\right)\\\\& =-144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & \\approx|-144|\\cdot\\delta A\\\\ & =144\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519687 :question/problem "![](images/56/56e82ecf0aa2a54d7281882c095f3c2b081f719cf3398be20155301d0f0361a0.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-9,0)$ and $(9,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-9,0)$ and $Y(9,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-9)-9)^2+(0-0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-18)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-9+9}{2},\\frac{0+0}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=9$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(9)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519688 :question/problem "![](images/95/95467ab2e67bc1e188a5783ef201859c4e1216f071d6dc21b44adafcba8195c4.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-5,-4)$ and $(-1,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-5,-4)$ and $Y(-1,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-5)-(-1))^2+((-4)-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 13} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-5+(-1)}{2},\\frac{-4+2}{2})=(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{13}$ and center $(a,b)=(-3,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{13})^2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519689 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4afda0c1af30967941c20c9f70ce926a04e029eedfa605eb3c20d62af8303947.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-9,-1)$ and $(3,-1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-9,-1)$ and $Y(3,-1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-9)-3)^2+((-1)-(-1))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{12}{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-9+3}{2},\\frac{-1+(-1)}{2})=(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=6$ and center $(a,b)=(-3,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & =(6)^2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519700 :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce6082e3e2563073817e143e8ce91b10c171d0e894d5ed3bea25d925c6fe718f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(7,0)$ and $(-7,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(7,0)$ and $Y(-7,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(7-(-7))^2+(0-0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(14)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{196+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{196} \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{14}{2}=7.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{7+(-7)}{2},\\frac{0+0}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=7$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(7)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519708 :question/problem "![](images/80/806745d02166fd14df9f563b58fb05a40bc0d9bc96460f57c51ba3e7c6f6d15a.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-4)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-4)$ and $Y(0,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-4)-4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-8)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-4+4}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(4)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519710 :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba4e8d2e873670ae8e941879bd54d1331af8c542b192d8a7a9407552326fbccc.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-8)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-8)$ and $Y(0,8),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-8)-8)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-16)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+256} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{256} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{16}{2}=8.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-8+8}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=8$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(8)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519720 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d851d51fea696fe66461a27557f85d9182ac476578923905d113a2fa28237a4.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(0,3)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-3)$ and $Y(0,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-3)-3)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-3+3}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=3$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(3)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519726 :question/problem "![](images/16/16fe72490f7df0dcdd1b3ab78809da1091eccb69a3dda98e3bef08635af322ed.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(4,2)$ and $(4,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(4,2)$ and $Y(4,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(4-4)^2+(2-(-2))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{4+4}{2},\\frac{2+(-2)}{2})=(4,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(4,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519739 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 12 y+\\left(24 x + 32 y\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\left(21 x + 22 y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =44 (1) + 42 (2) \\\\ & =48+\\left(80\\right) \\\\ & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519742 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(4 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(4 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 24 y+\\left(24 x + 18 y\\right) \\\\  & =42 \\left(x + y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =42 (1) + 42 (2) \\\\ & =60+\\left(66\\right) \\\\ & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519747 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x^{2} e^{3 y}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\ln{\\left(3 x + y \\right)})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(3 x^{2} e^{3 y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3 x + y}+\\left(9 x^{2} e^{3 y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(9 x^{3} e^{3 y} + 3 x^{2} y e^{3 y} + 1\\right)}{3 x + y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,0) & =\\frac{3 \\left(3 (0) (2)^{2} e^{3 (0)} + 9 (2)^{3} e^{3 (0)} + 1\\right)}{(0) + 3 (2)} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(36\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{73}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519751 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 12 y+\\left(12 x + 18 y\\right) \\\\  & =30 \\left(x + y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =30 (1) + 30 (2) \\\\ & =48+\\left(42\\right) \\\\ & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519754 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(4 x + y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(4 x + y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =32 x + 8 y+\\left(24 x + 32 y\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\left(7 x + 5 y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =40 (1) + 56 (2) \\\\ & =72+\\left(80\\right) \\\\ & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519760 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(3 \\ln{\\left(x - y \\right)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x + y}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{x - y}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{x + 5 y}{\\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(3,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(3,1) & =- \\frac{5 (1) + (3)}{\\left(- (1) + (3)\\right) \\left((1) + (3)\\right)} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519776 :question/problem "![](images/26/26fffdfc736d59a285ec7beb916417657c50a48494eace96e0db5d70cd7ce522.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0a/0a8351f5cdfa59df7ec8a328c18b6e83342796728e11ff26911ce661eda1513b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519789 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=3$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=1$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1-\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519796 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 9 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519799 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 12 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519800 :question/problem "![](images/26/26fffdfc736d59a285ec7beb916417657c50a48494eace96e0db5d70cd7ce522.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0a/0a8351f5cdfa59df7ec8a328c18b6e83342796728e11ff26911ce661eda1513b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 3 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 3 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 x y - 3 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 x y - 3 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519811 :question/problem "![](images/44/44c489cbdf4665cca7fc7b8a331f5ccf4d72f8d15577f58c45497c58d5817bf3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c4cf3906e5af81f6cb071bae5b594861351987934fe6d51443c09e17982b2204.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=12 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 12 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519814 :question/problem "![](images/44/44c489cbdf4665cca7fc7b8a331f5ccf4d72f8d15577f58c45497c58d5817bf3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=3$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c4cf3906e5af81f6cb071bae5b594861351987934fe6d51443c09e17982b2204.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519817 :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1dc30e767ab459dd4625582c1d06a14f506a8600b3b47ff0cf650e160d5f9d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/88/888f97120e097c46a7dfdcfb137fbae11b71e1606ade407f7c644468de4ede2c.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 6 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519818 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519833 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c7fc0c4d92c68dad3d8607f25f7be80d79ca7b2d412b67b0942a669725e3d36.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=4$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4b3e6446a0ae02a08e91d527f402287151ddb3f39c7b744503430cb9697e03a.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(4-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 5,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(3\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519921 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3f6e03956e6002db05950b10d9608be87dd45a4bb75b3822e1ab754981eb19d.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2 x y^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(\\frac{1}{2},2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x y^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) t^{6} \\\\  & =2 t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 x y^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{5}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=t^{4} + 1,$ $\\text{d}u=4 t^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{2}^{17}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(17\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519933 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=e^{\\frac{y}{2}}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=e^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨e^{\\frac{t}{2}},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{\\frac{t}{2}})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{e^{\\frac{t}{2}}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{e^{\\frac{t}{2}}}{2}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 \\left(e^{\\frac{t}{2}}\\right)^{6} e^{- 2 t} \\\\  & =2 e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\sqrt{e^{t} + 4} e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=e^{t} + 4,$ $\\text{d}u=e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\sqrt{e^{t} + 4} e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =1{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[1\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(6\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4 \\sqrt{6}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3} + 4 \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519936 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $y^2=6x$ from $(\\frac{7}{6},\\sqrt{7})$ to $(\\frac{8}{3},4).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny^2=6x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t^{2}}{6},\\,t⟩,\\,\\sqrt{7}\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $\\sqrt{7}\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{6}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{6 x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{6 \\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{6}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\left(t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\frac{t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=t^{2} + 9,$ $\\text{d}u=2 t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\frac{t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{16}^{25}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right]_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(25\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(16\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{125}{9}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{9}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519944 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 1$ from $(5,2)$ to $(7,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 1,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 t \\\\  & =2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[\\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{3^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =5 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519947 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 1$ from $(5,2)$ to $(11,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 1,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 t \\\\  & =2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[\\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{5^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{25}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =21 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519949 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 t \\\\  & =8 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\left(8 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[4 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{3^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =20 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519958 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca91ec7d1e638b49ab7d4334397c5c7d383b7df0bce022c0e481b857ae2e1b95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(12,2 \\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{3}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2 t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{6 x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{6 \\left(t^{2}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =6 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}\\left(6 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}6 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1,$ $\\text{d}u=8 t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}6 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{5}^{49}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{49}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(49\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{343}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{343}{2} - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519969 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}6 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(4,1)$ to $(6,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6 t \\\\  & =6 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(6 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[3 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{2^2}{2}-\\frac{1^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6 \\sqrt{5}\\left(2-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =9 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519977 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(12,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 t \\\\  & =8 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\left(8 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[4 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{5^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{25}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =84 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519979 :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad80637c8c61ee847bd385c39fb0eec5fc1b14ee4f9d6c1796fb31baff48c016.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 \\sqrt{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(2 \\sqrt{5},5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 \\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 \\sqrt{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\sqrt{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 x y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 \\left(2 \\sqrt{t}\\right) t \\\\  & =16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\left(16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}16 t \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4 t + 4,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}16 t \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =8{\\int }_{8}^{24}\\frac{u-4}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{24}\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2} - 2 \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2}{5}u^{5/2}-(4)\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right)\\right]_{8}^{24} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5} - \\frac{4 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{8}^{24} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(24\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(24\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(8\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(8\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{1152 \\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- 64 \\sqrt{6}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{128 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
